# Supabase / Database Layer Guardrails

## Scope
- Apply this rule to Supabase and SQL work: schema, migrations, roles, grants, and RLS policies.
- Treat any DB task as safety-critical, especially for production data and access control.

## Mandatory workflow
- First provide a SQL change plan, then implement migration(s).
- Explain expected impact before changing schema, roles, grants, or policies.
- Apply database changes only via migration files; avoid ad-hoc manual edits as the main path.

## Schema design rules
- Prefer additive and backward-compatible changes.
- Use clear naming, explicit data types, and sensible defaults.
- Add primary keys, required foreign keys, and indexes for frequent filters/joins.
- For nullable vs not-null decisions, document why and how existing rows are handled.

## Migration rules
- Every DB change must be represented by a migration file.
- Keep migrations small, reviewable, and reversible where possible.
- Split high-risk changes into stages (prepare -> migrate data -> enforce constraints).
- Do not combine unrelated schema, role, and policy changes in one migration.

## Roles and access model
- Maintain role separation: `admin`, `editor`, `participant`.
- Follow least privilege for all roles.
- Never grant broad access to `anon`; grant only explicitly required read access.
- Re-check role permissions when adding new tables or columns.

## RLS policy rules
- For private tables, RLS must be enabled and validated.
- Do not modify existing RLS policies blindly; review current policies and access paths first.
- For every policy change, specify: who can select/insert/update/delete and why.
- Validate policies against role scenarios (`admin`, `editor`, `participant`, `anon`).

## Destructive changes protection (ask first)
- Do not drop tables, columns, constraints, indexes, or policies without explicit user confirmation.
- Do not run data-destructive SQL (mass delete/truncate/backfill overwrite) without explicit approval.
- If destructive change is approved, provide backup/rollback plan and blast-radius explanation first.

## Safe SQL change checklist
- Confirm affected tables, columns, indexes, and policies.
- Confirm impact on existing queries, API responses, and auth/RLS behavior.
- Confirm migration order and data compatibility.
- Confirm how to verify after apply (smoke queries and role-based access checks).
